insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 1, 'BONGA is a butane cloud of 3000 kg with a heat of combustion of 45700000 J/kg, and the TNT blast energy is stated as 4600000 J/kg. What TNT equivalent mass does `tntEquivalentMass` return at a yield factor of 0.1?', '["596.086957 kg, which is the row printed for a yield factor of 0.02", "5960.869565 kg, which is the row printed for a yield factor of 0.2", "2980.434783 kg", "1192.173913 kg, which is the row printed for 0.04"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s model string is Q_TNT = alpha_e Qf Emf / Em_TNT and the digest prints 2980.434783 kg against a yield factor of 0.1. 596.086957, 1192.173913 and 5960.869565 kg are the rows for 0.02, 0.04 and 0.2: each is the right arithmetic at a different stated yield, and the TNT mass is linear in the yield, so reading the wrong row moves the answer in proportion.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 2, 'On the same BONGA cloud, which stated yield factor produces the TNT equivalent mass of 1192.173913 kg?', '["0.04", "0.02", "0.1", "0.2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest''s table pairs 1192.173913 kg with a yield factor of 0.04. The yield of 0.02 gives 596.086957 kg, 0.1 gives 2980.434783 kg and 0.2 gives 5960.869565 kg. The yield is the caller''s input; the Yellow Book reports 0.02 to 0.2 in use, and the engine carries no default for it.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 3, 'An analyst types the TNT blast energy in kJ/kg instead of J/kg. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["A result with a TNT mass one thousand times too large, since the engine never converts a unit it was not asked to convert", "A refusal naming `yieldFactor`, because the slip is caught as a yield outside the band of 0.02 to 0.2 the Yellow Book reports in use", "A result carrying a warning that the energy lies outside the Yellow Book''s cited 4.19e6 to 4.65e6 J/kg", "A refusal naming `tntBlastEnergyJKg`: \"tntBlastEnergyJKg: must be the TNT blast energy in J/kg, between 4.0e6 and 5.0e6 (the YB cites 4.19e6 to 4.65e6)\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 3 tables this refusal: the TNT blast energy is refused outside 4.0e6 to 5.0e6 J/kg precisely to catch a units slip, and the field named is `tntBlastEnergyJKg`. No result comes back, so neither a scaled up TNT mass nor a warning is returned. The yield field is a different refusal with its own message.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 4, 'A yield factor is typed as a percentage, 10, where the engine expects a fraction. Which message does the engine return?', '["\"tntBlastEnergyJKg: must be the TNT blast energy in J/kg, between 4.0e6 and 5.0e6 (the YB cites 4.19e6 to 4.65e6)\"", "\"yieldFactor: the TNT equivalency (yield) must lie in (0, 1]: the YB reports 0.02 to 0.2 in use\"", "\"dischargeCoefficient: must lie in (0, 1]: the YB recommends 0.62 for a sharp orifice\"", "\"radiativeFraction: must lie in (0, 1]: the YB gives 0.1 to 0.4\""]'::jsonb, 1, 'The yield must lie in (0, 1], so 10 is refused on the field `yieldFactor` with the message quoted. The TNT energy message belongs to a units slip in the blast energy. The discharge coefficient and radiative fraction messages share the (0, 1] form but belong to `liquidOrificeDischarge` and `surfaceEmissivePower`, and the engine names the field that failed.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 5, 'Section 32 plants the same mistake in the engine and its oracle and asks what still catches it. What does it say catches a mistake in the TNT equivalence?', '["The published conference column, which the golden labels as a published case and reproduces within 1.70e-4", "Nothing: the transcription alone, so the TNT equivalence is taught and never graded", "The OSD/30 printed lethal doses, read against the engine to the digits the table prints", "The Yellow Book worked cases, together with the oracle''s own nozzle maximisation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The section 32 row for the TNT equivalence reads ''nothing: the transcription alone'' and ''no'' under graded. The conference column stands behind Kinney and Graham, the OSD/30 lethal doses behind the thermal presets, and the Yellow Book worked cases with the nozzle maximisation behind the outflow through a hole. That is why every capstone that needs a TNT mass STATES it.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 6, 'BONGA''s stated charge is 500 kg of TNT. What scaled distance does the engine report for a target 100 m away?', '["6.299605 m/kg^(1/3), the Z of the 50 m row", "25.198421 m/kg^(1/3), the Z of the 200 m row", "2.519842 m/kg^(1/3)", "12.599210 m/kg^(1/3)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s model string is "Hopkinson-Cranz cube-root scaling, Z = R / W^(1/3)", and the 100 m row of BONGA''s 500 kg table prints Z = 12.599210. The values 2.519842, 6.299605 and 25.198421 are the same charge at 20, 50 and 200 m, which scale linearly with the distance because the charge is held.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 7, 'Under Hopkinson-Cranz scaling, at what distance does a charge eight times heavier give the same peak overpressure as the original charge?', '["At twice the distance, because Z = R / W^(1/3) and the cube root of the charge ratio is two", "At eight times the distance, since the overpressure would then scale in direct proportion to the charge mass", "At the square root of eight times the distance, as a square root scaling law would give", "At the same distance, because the overpressure ratio is independent of the charge"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two charges give the same overpressure at the same Z, and Z divides the distance by the cube root of the charge. A charge eight times heavier therefore meets the same Z at twice the distance. Proportional scaling and square root scaling are wrong laws for this model, and holding the distance fixed raises the overpressure because Z falls.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 8, 'The digest runs eight times BONGA''s 500 kg charge at twice 100 m. What overpressure does the engine print for that case?', '["3427.919093 Pa, the original 500 kg charge at the 200 m row", "19626.657230 Pa, which is the original charge at the 50 m row", "7457.699887 Pa", "9985.363723 Pa"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Eight times the charge at twice the distance has the same Z as the original charge at 100 m, so the engine gives 7457.699887 Pa both ways. 3427.919093 Pa is the original charge at 200 m, which forgets to scale the charge. 19626.657230 Pa is the 50 m row, and 9985.363723 Pa is the ratio table at Z = 10, a nearby Z that is not this one.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 9, 'In the digest''s table of the ratio over ambient against Z, what overpressure in Pa does the Kinney and Graham fit give at Z = 5?', '["9985.363723 Pa", "207926.999232 Pa", "0.288558 Pa, the fit''s ratio read as a pressure", "29238.106780 Pa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At Z = 5 the fit''s ratio is 0.288558 and the overpressure is 29238.106780 Pa. Reporting 0.288558 as a pressure forgets that the fit returns ps/pa and has to be multiplied by the ambient. 207926.999232 Pa and 9985.363723 Pa are the Z = 2 and Z = 10 rows.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 10, 'The Kinney and Graham fit returns 9.955978 at Z = 1. What is that number?', '["The peak side-on overpressure over the ambient, a ratio, which the engine reports as 1008789.503793 Pa", "The peak side-on overpressure in kPa, which the engine then multiplies by one thousand to report it in Pa", "The overpressure in psig, which the engine converts with `PA_PER_PSI` before it reports the figure in Pa", "The overpressure in bar, a convenient unit close to the charge, which the engine rescales before it reports Pa"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s model string gives ps/pa, the overpressure divided by the ambient pressure, and the table prints the ratio 9.955978 beside 1008789.503793 Pa at Z = 1. The ratio carries no unit, so reading it as kPa, psig or bar invents a conversion the engine never makes. `PA_PER_PSI` belongs to the overpressure probit.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 11, 'What does the engine''s validation record say about the range Z from 0.05 to 40 over which the Kinney and Graham fit is used?', '["It is printed by Kinney and Graham (1985) with the fit, and the engine copies it without change from their edition", "It is the span the Yellow Book states for the TNT equivalence method as a whole", "It is a judgement: the sources it read print no range for the fit itself, and the span is borrowed from the Kingery-Bulmash compilation", "It is fixed by the conference column, whose five published cases run from Z = 0.05 up to 40"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 24 says the validation record calls the range a JUDGEMENT, and the engine''s range string reads "Z in [0.05, 40] m/kg^(1/3) (judgement; see findings)". The sources read print no range for Kinney and Graham itself, so it is neither printed with the fit nor stated by the Yellow Book, and the conference column''s five cases do not define a span.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 12, 'A call asks `kinneyGrahamOverpressure` for a scaled distance beyond 40 m/kg^(1/3). What comes back?', '["A result carrying a warning to treat it as an extrapolation, the way the Briggs sigmas behave outside 100 m to 10 km", "A refusal naming `scaledDistanceMKg13`: \"scaledDistanceMKg13: Z lies outside 0.05 to 40 m/kg^(1/3), the range this fit is used over\"", "The overpressure at the edge of the range, 2121.136283 Pa, since the engine clamps Z to 40", "A result with the state BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE and a distance of null"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses outside its judged range and names the field `scaledDistanceMKg13`. The Briggs sigmas are the function that warns and still returns, and nothing in the explosions section clamps Z. BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE is a state of the plume and heat flux distance searches.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 13, 'BONGA''s 500 kg charge is evaluated at 300 m, its farthest printed row. What overpressure does the engine give there?', '["2247.931733 Pa", "2121.136283 Pa, which is the value at the Z = 40 edge of the ratio table", "3427.919093 Pa, which is printed for the same charge at the 200 m row", "4393.409670 Pa, which the ratio table gives at Z = 20"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 300 m row prints Z = 37.797631 and 2247.931733 Pa, just inside the judged range. 2121.136283 Pa is Z = 40, 3427.919093 Pa is the 200 m row and 4393.409670 Pa is Z = 20: each belongs to a different scaled distance.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 14, 'At a yield factor of 0.2 BONGA''s TNT equivalent mass, 5960.869565 kg, is larger than its fuel mass of 3000 kg. What in the engine''s model string makes that possible?', '["The engine adds the fuel mass and the TNT mass together before it reports Q_TNT, so the charge that the blast fit takes always exceeds the fuel", "The yield factor is applied twice, once to the fuel mass and once to the heat of combustion", "The engine multiplies the TNT mass by the ambient pressure of 101325 Pa before it reports the charge", "The fuel''s heat of combustion, 45700000 J/kg, is much larger than the TNT blast energy, 4600000 J/kg, and their ratio multiplies the yield"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Q_TNT = alpha_e Qf Emf / Em_TNT, so the ratio of heats, 45700000 over 4600000 J/kg, multiplies the yield and the fuel mass. Nothing is added, the yield enters once, and the ambient pressure appears only in turning the fit''s ratio into pascals.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-tnt-and-the-scaled-distance', 15, 'Every basis block names its source. Which source string does the Kinney and Graham overpressure carry, verbatim, from `CONSEQUENCE_SOURCES`?', '["TNO Yellow Book CPR 14E (2005), the source that fixes the TNT equivalence and the outflow through a hole", "UK HSE SPC/Tech/OSD/30, Indicative human vulnerability to the hazardous agents present offshore", "Kinney and Graham (1985) Explosive Shocks in Air, 2nd ed.; as printed by Guzas and Earls (2010) eq. 5", "TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999), the source of the probit to probability table"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The KG key in `CONSEQUENCE_SOURCES` reads as quoted and fixes the free air peak side-on overpressure closed form. The Yellow Book fixes the TNT equivalence, OSD/30 the thermal, toxic and overpressure probits, and the Purple Book the probit table and its toxic coefficients. The 2020 conference paper is the golden''s secondary evidence and is no source key.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 1, '`distanceForOverpressure` is asked where BONGA''s 500 kg of TNT gives 100000 Pa. What distance does it return?', '["30.090623 m, the distance returned for a target of 50000 Pa", "21.822266 m", "20 m", "49.426650 m, the distance returned for a target of 20000 Pa"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The inverse table prints 21.822266 m, at Z = 2.749433, for 100000 Pa. 30.090623 m and 49.426650 m answer the 50000 and 20000 Pa targets. 20 m is a stated row of the forward table, where the overpressure is 121853.141070 Pa, so it lies closer in than the 100000 Pa distance.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 2, 'For a target of 10000 Pa on the same charge, which scaled distance, in m/kg^(1/3), does the bisection settle on?', '["10, since the ratio table prints Z = 10 against the nearest overpressure to the target", "6.227368, the Z the bisection settles on for 20000 Pa", "17.786268, the Z the bisection settles on for 5000 Pa", "9.988894"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The inverse table prints Z = 9.988894 for 10000 Pa, giving 79.281907 m. Z = 10 gives 9985.363723 Pa, a little below the target, so the root sits just inside it. 6.227368 and 17.786268 are the 20000 and 5000 Pa rows.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 3, 'How does the engine invert the Kinney and Graham fit to find a distance, in its own method string?', '["\"inverse of the Kinney and Graham fit by bisection on Z\"", "An algebraic inverse of the closed form, solved exactly for Z and then multiplied by the cube root of the charge", "A lookup on the Kingery-Bulmash curves, which the engine interpolates between the printed points", "A Newton iteration on the distance in m, holding the charge and the ambient fixed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s method is the bisection quoted, run on Z, after which the distance follows from Z times the cube root of the charge. The fit has no algebraic inverse in the engine, the Kingery-Bulmash fits are not in the engine at all (section 31), and no Newton step on the distance is used.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 4, 'The inverse table puts 50000 Pa at 30.090623 m for 500 kg. Fed back into the forward fit at that distance, what overpressure does the engine return?', '["19626.657230 Pa, the overpressure the forward table prints at 50 m", "121853.141070 Pa, the overpressure the forward table prints at the 20 m row", "50000 Pa, because every row of the inverse table round trips through the forward fit", "100000 Pa, since the bisection brackets the root from above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 25 states that every row round trips through the forward fit, so 30.090623 m returns the 50000 Pa it was searched for, to within the rounding of the printed distance. 19626.657230 Pa and 121853.141070 Pa are the forward rows at 50 m and 20 m, and 100000 Pa is another target altogether.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 5, 'The 500 kg charge gives 2121.136283 Pa at the Z = 40 edge of the judged range. A target below that overpressure is passed to `distanceForOverpressure`. What happens?', '["It returns the distance at Z = 40 with the state BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE, which is the way the plume search reports a target it cannot reach", "It returns a distance beyond Z = 40 with a warning that the result is an extrapolation of the fit", "It is refused on `overpressurePa`, the field the inverse names when no Z inside the judged range can give the target", "It is refused on `scaledDistanceMKg13`, the field the forward fit names outside its range"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The inverse refuses an overpressure the fit cannot give inside its range, on the field `overpressurePa`, in the engine''s words "overpressurePa: lies outside the overpressures the fit gives over Z = 0.05 to 40 m/kg^(1/3)". It has no BEYOND_SEARCH_RANGE state and no extrapolation warning; those belong to the distance searches and to the Briggs sigmas. `scaledDistanceMKg13` is the forward function''s field.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 6, 'The 2020 conference paper PRINTS the Kinney and Graham formula with two constants that its own column does not follow. Which constants does the engine use?', '["808 and 0.048, which the paper''s column follows and two other sources print", "800 and 0.049, as the paper prints them in its formula, since the golden is labelled PUBLISHED from that paper", "808 and 0.049, taking the leading constant from the column and the break from the printed formula", "800 and 0.048"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 24: the paper prints 800 and 0.049, but its column follows 808 and 0.048, and those are the constants in the engine''s model string. Using the printed pair, or either mixed pair, would fail to reproduce the column that is the evidence.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 7, 'Why does the golden label the conference column PUBLISHED when the paper misprints its own formula?', '["Because the engine uses the printed formula and the column was corrected to agree with it before the golden was written", "Because Kinney and Graham (1985) print the same five values in their own worked table of the fit", "Because all five cases agree with the engine to better than 3.09e-6 relative, the smallest of the five differences", "Because the column is the evidence: its numbers reproduce through the engine, whatever the printed formula says"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says the column is the evidence and the golden is PUBLISHED because its numbers reproduce. The engine does not use the printed formula, the five values come from the 2020 paper''s table, and 3.09e-6 is the difference for one case only; cbu-0.5kg-5m sits at 1.70e-4.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 8, 'The conference case cbu-1kg-5m prints 29.24 kPa and the engine gives 29.238107 kPa. Which row of the ratio table against Z does that case sit on?', '["The Z = 10 row, 9985.363723 Pa, because the distance is divided by the half kilogram of the charge", "The Z = 5 row, 29238.106780 Pa, because a 1 kg charge has a cube root of one", "The Z = 2 row, 207926.999232 Pa, the nearest printed Z to the square root of five", "The Z = 1 row, 1008789.503793 Pa, since the charge is 1 kg"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Z = R / W^(1/3), and 5 m over the cube root of 1 kg is 5, where the ratio table prints 29238.106780 Pa, the same figure as 29.238107 kPa. The other rows come from a wrong scaling: halving, a square root, or reading the charge as the scaled distance.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 9, 'The conference case cbu-0.5kg-5m reads 19.626657 kPa, which matches BONGA''s 500 kg at 50 m, 19626.657230 Pa. Why do the two agree?', '["They share the scaled distance 6.299605 m/kg^(1/3): a thousand times the charge at ten times the distance", "The conference paper used BONGA''s charge and scaled it down by one thousand for its table", "The two cases were run at different ambients that happen to cancel the difference in charge", "The engine rounds every overpressure near that value to one shared printed figure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cube root scaling makes the overpressure a function of Z alone, and both cases sit at Z = 6.299605. The paper''s cases are its own, the golden is at 101.325 kPa, and the engine prints six decimals rather than rounding into bands.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 10, 'Among the five conference cases, which carries the largest relative difference between the engine and the printed figure?', '["cbu-1kg-5m, which the digest prints at 6.47e-5", "cbu-0.1kg-2m, which the digest prints at 5.73e-5", "cbu-0.1kg-1m, which the digest prints at 3.09e-6", "cbu-0.5kg-5m, at 1.70e-4"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The column''s relative differences are 6.47e-5, 1.70e-4, 3.34e-5, 5.73e-5 and 3.09e-6, so cbu-0.5kg-5m, printed 19.63 against 19.626657 kPa, is the largest. The printed values carry two or three decimals, which is what sets the size of each difference.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 11, 'A charge bursts at the ground and its shock reflects. What does the engine do about the reflection?', '["It doubles the overpressure for any charge flagged as a ground burst before it reports it", "Nothing in the fit: the fit is a free air burst, and the engine header leaves the ground burst to the caller''s choice of charge weight", "It adds an image charge below the ground, the way the Gaussian plume adds an image source at minus h", "It switches to the Kingery-Bulmash hemispherical fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 25: the fit is a FREE AIR burst of TNT; a burst at the ground reflects its shock and is not modelled, and the header says that is the caller''s choice of charge weight. There is no doubling flag and no image charge (the image source is the plume''s reflection), and Kingery-Bulmash is not in the engine.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 12, 'A vapour cloud explodes in a congested process area. What does this engine carry for the difference between that cloud and a TNT charge?', '["The TNO multi-energy method, whose blast curves the engine reads for a stated level of congestion", "A congestion factor applied to the Kinney and Graham ratio", "Only the yield factor, which carries the whole difference; the TNO multi-energy method, which treats congestion, is not in the engine", "A second heat of combustion for the cloud, which the engine uses in place of the TNT blast energy of 4600000 J/kg"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A vapour cloud is not TNT, and section 25 says the yield factor carries the whole difference. The multi-energy method is named in section 31 as out of the engine because its charts are graphical and no published curve fit was found, so no congestion factor exists. The TNT blast energy is always the denominator.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 13, 'On BONGA''s forward table for 500 kg, which stated distance gives 3427.919093 Pa?', '["100 m, where Z is 12.599210", "300 m, the farthest row printed", "50 m, where Z is 6.299605", "200 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 200 m row prints Z = 25.198421 and 3427.919093 Pa. The 50 m and 100 m rows are closer and higher, 19626.657230 and 7457.699887 Pa, and the 300 m row is farther and lower, 2247.931733 Pa.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 14, 'The forward table puts BONGA''s 20 m row at 121853.141070 Pa, and the inverse table puts 100000 Pa at 21.822266 m. How do the two agree?', '["They are consistent: 20 m is closer in than 21.822266 m, so it sees more than 100000 Pa", "They disagree, which shows the bisection stops before it converges on the root", "They disagree because the forward and inverse functions use different constants", "They are consistent only because the forward table uses a rounded scaled distance of 2.519842"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The overpressure falls with distance, so a target closer than the 100000 Pa distance sees a higher overpressure, as 121853.141070 Pa does. The inverse bisects on the same fit, rows round trip, and the forward fit does not depend on the six decimal rounding of Z printed in the table.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-blast-field', 15, 'The conference column is computed at what ambient, and how does the engine''s validation record rank that table as evidence?', '["At an ambient of 100000 Pa, and it ranks the table above every Yellow Book case because five values reproduce", "At an ambient of 101.325 kPa, and it names the table as secondary", "At the stated ambient of each case, printed beside it", "At 6894.757293168361 Pa, and as a primary source"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 24 prints the column at an ambient of 101.325 kPa and says the validation record names the table as secondary. 100000 Pa is one of BONGA''s targets, the table prints no per case ambient, and 6894.757293168361 is `PA_PER_PSI`, a pascal count per psi.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 1, 'The engine''s model string is "P = Phi(Y - 5), standard normal CDF". What probability does it return for a probit of 6?', '["0.158655, the probability of a probit of 4", "0.841345", "0.977250", "0.500000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A probit is a normal deviate shifted by five, so Y = 6 is one standard deviation above the mean and the ladder prints 0.841345. 0.158655 is Y = 4, one below; 0.977250 is Y = 7; 0.500000 is Y = 5, where Phi(0) is one half.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 2, 'The Purple Book''s Table 5.1 prints 3.36 for a probability of 0.05, and the engine''s inverse gives 3.355147. Why does the golden count that cell as reproduced?', '["The golden allows 0.002 absolute on every probit cell, the tolerance it uses for the carbon monoxide probability", "The table prints to two decimals, and 3.355147 rounds to the printed 3.36", "The engine rounds its own inverse to two decimals before the comparison with the printed Table 5.1 cell is made", "The golden accepts any cell within one percent relative"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Table 5.1 prints each probit to two decimals, and the engine''s inverse reproduces 97 of its 97 cells at the printed two decimals; 3.355147 rounds to 3.36. The 0.002 absolute tolerance belongs to the Purple Book''s carbon monoxide probability read off the table, the engine does not round its output, and no one percent rule applies here.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 3, 'Two cells of Table 5.1 sit on a rounding edge. What are they, and how does the golden treat them?', '["Probability 0.01, printed 2.67 against 2.673653, and 0.99, printed 7.33 against 7.326347; kept apart as errata", "Probability 0.25, printed 4.33 against 4.325510, and 0.75, printed 5.67 against 5.674490; counted as errata of the Purple Book", "Probability 0.12, printed 3.82 against 3.825013, and 0.88, printed 6.18 against 6.174987; within 0.006 of the printed digits, kept apart", "Probability 0.1 and 0.9, both dropped from the golden"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 26 names 0.12 and 0.88: their values, 3.825013 and 6.174987, sit on a rounding edge within 0.006 of the printed digits, so the golden keeps them apart. The 0.01, 0.99, 0.25 and 0.75 cells reproduce to two decimals and are in the 97, and the 0.1 and 0.9 cells are there too.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 4, '`probit` computes Y = a + b ln(V) for any coefficients. What does ln mean here?', '["The natural logarithm, as the engine''s model string \"Y = a + b ln(V); P = Phi(Y - 5)\" states", "The logarithm to base 10, as a probit fitted on a log scale of the exposure would read on a printed chart", "The natural logarithm of V divided by 1e4, whatever the preset''s unit", "The logarithm to base 2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 26 says the logarithm is NATURAL. A base 10 log would change every coefficient''s meaning, and the division by 1e4 belongs only to the W/m2 form of Eisenberg, where it converts the unit of the intensity. No base 2 form appears.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 5, 'What coefficients and intensity unit does the engine''s eisenberg thermal preset carry?', '["a = -14.9, b = 2.56, with the intensity in kW/m2", "a = -12.8, b = 2.56, with the intensity in kW/m2, as OSD/30 Table 17 prints them", "a = -36.38, b = 2.56, with the intensity in W/m2, the form of eq. 5.4", "a = -10.7, b = 1.99, with the intensity in kW/m2, as OSD/30 Table 17 prints them"]'::jsonb, 0, '`THERMAL_PROBITS` lists eisenberg as -14.9 and 2.56 in kW/m2. -12.8 with 2.56 is tsao-perry, -36.38 with 2.56 in W/m2 is purple-book (eq. 5.4), and -10.7 with 1.99 is lees. Each is named by its source so the presets cannot be confused.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 6, 'A person is exposed to 20000 W/m2 for 20 s. What lethality probability does the eisenberg preset give?', '["0.537647, the tsao-perry figure for this exposure", "0.537040, the purple-book figure for this exposure", "0.022455", "0.036742"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The thermal preset table prints 0.022455 under Eisenberg for 20000 W/m2 for 20 s. Tsao and Perry gives 0.537647 and the Purple Book 0.537040, with Lees at 0.036742. The same exposure reads as a few percent under one preset and most of the people exposed under another, which is why a result is quoted with its preset.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 7, 'At 20000 W/m2 for 20 s the Eisenberg probit is 2.994507 and the Tsao and Perry probit is 5.094507, derived 2.100000 higher. Where does that fixed gap come from?', '["Tsao and Perry takes the intensity in W/m2, so its thermal dose is larger by the conversion from kW/m2", "Tsao and Perry raises the intensity to the power 1.99 where Eisenberg uses four thirds", "Tsao and Perry takes the exposure time in minutes where Eisenberg uses seconds, so the time term is larger", "Both presets have b = 2.56 and the same thermal dose, so the gap is the difference in a, -12.8 against -14.9"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both presets are Y = a + 2.56 ln(t I^(4/3)) with I in kW/m2 and t in seconds, so for one thermal dose, 1085.767047 s (kW/m2)^(4/3), the probits differ by the difference in a. Both take kW/m2, 1.99 is Lees''s b and no exponent, and minutes belong to the toxic probits.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 8, 'Which preset is the Purple Book heat probit, -36.38 + 2.56 ln(Q^(4/3) t) with Q in W/m2, rewritten from?', '["Eisenberg, since the Purple Book is a TNO source and the TNO heat probit is Eisenberg''s form", "Tsao and Perry: -12.8 - 2.56 ln(1e4) is -36.378", "Lees, rewritten into W/m2 with its own b", "The TNO row of OSD/30 Table 17, a = -15.3 and b = 3.02"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 27: NOT EVERY TNO HEAT PROBIT IS EISENBERG. Shifting Tsao and Perry into W/m2 subtracts 2.56 ln(1e4) from -12.8 and gives -36.378, the Purple Book''s constant; that is why its probability at 20000 W/m2 for 20 s, 0.537040, sits beside Tsao and Perry''s 0.537647. Lees has b = 1.99, and the TNO row is an erratum that is no preset.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 9, 'OSD/30 also writes Eisenberg as -14.9 + 2.56 ln(t q^(4/3) / 1e4) with q in W/m2. At 10000 W/m2 for 30 s the engine gives 1.666556. Why is that the same probit as the kW/m2 form?', '["Because the engine converts every preset to W/m2 internally and drops the 1e4 as a rounding term of no consequence", "Because the four thirds power cancels the thousand between the units exactly", "Because the two forms differ only at exposures above 30 s", "Because (1000 I)^(4/3) is 1e4 I^(4/3), so dividing by 1e4 turns a W/m2 thermal dose back into the kW/m2 one"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The factor 1000 between the units becomes 1000^(4/3) = 1e4 inside the thermal dose, and dividing by 1e4 undoes it, so both forms give 1.666556 at the golden case. The engine feeds each preset the heat flux in W/m2 and converts it to that preset''s unit; nothing is dropped, and the forms agree at every exposure.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 10, 'OSD/30 Table 17 prints Eisenberg''s lethal dose for one percent as 960 (kW/m2)^(4/3) s. What does the engine give?', '["957.866291", "421.742820", "2376.626918", "829.172848"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s Eisenberg one percent lethal dose is 957.866291 against the printed 960. 421.742820 and 829.172848 are the Tsao and Perry and Lees one percent figures, and 2376.626918 is Eisenberg at fifty percent, printed as 2380.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 11, 'OSD/30 Table 17 carries a TNO row, a = -15.3 and b = 3.02, printing lethal doses of 389 and 841. Why is it no preset in the engine?', '["It is the Purple Book form, which the engine already carries as purple-book with its own a and b", "Its coefficients take the intensity in W/m2, which the engine''s thermal presets do not accept", "Its printed lethal doses do not follow from its own coefficients (the golden computes 384.344943 and 830.355817), and the source does not say which is wrong", "It reproduces exactly but duplicates Eisenberg, so it was merged into that preset"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 27 records the erratum: from the row''s coefficients the golden computes 384.344943 and 830.355817 against the printed 389 and 841, so the row is not a preset. Asked for it by name the engine refuses: "coefficients: unknown preset ''tno''; one of eisenberg, tsao-perry, lees, purple-book, or give { a, b } explicitly". The Purple Book form is Tsao and Perry, the purple-book preset takes W/m2 itself, and the row does not reproduce.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 12, 'The hsc overpressure probit is Y = 1.47 + 1.37 ln(P) with P in psig. What probability does the engine give at 20 psig?', '["0.353660, the probability printed at 10 psig", "0.717068", "0.936216, the probability printed at 40 psig", "0.500000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The overpressure probit table prints a probit of 5.574153 and a probability of 0.717068 at 20 psig, which the engine takes as 137895.145863 Pa and converts with `PA_PER_PSI`. 0.353660 and 0.936216 are the 10 and 40 psig rows, and one half is reached near 13.152901 psig.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 13, 'An analyst feeds the hsc probit the overpressure in Pa where the coefficients expect psig, bypassing the engine''s conversion. What goes wrong?', '["The value inside the logarithm is `PA_PER_PSI` times too large, 6894.757293168361 times, so the probit reads far too high", "Nothing, because the logarithm makes the probit insensitive to the unit of the overpressure", "The probit reads too low by the factor 101325, the ambient pressure", "The engine refuses on `overpressurePa` with the message it uses for a target outside the fit''s range"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 28: fed Pa where psig is due, the probit reads a number `PA_PER_PSI` times too large, and with b = 1.37 on a natural log that raises Y by a large fixed amount. The logarithm shifts the result; it does not remove the error. 101325 is `ATM_PA`, and the `overpressurePa` refusal belongs to `distanceForOverpressure`.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 14, 'OSD/30 Equation 4 prints 43.5 psig as the overpressure for a probability of 0.95. The engine gives 43.696383 psig. How does the validation record read that gap?', '["As a fault in the engine''s psig conversion, which it corrects by carrying `PA_PER_PSI` to more digits", "As the rounding of a two decimal table, the same effect as the Table 5.1 cells on a rounding edge", "As proof that the hsc preset should be refitted to the three printed points before a capstone grades it", "As an erratum in the source: the ninety-five percent point prints lower than the probit gives"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 28 says the ninety-five percent point prints lower than the probit gives, which the validation record lists as an erratum in the source; its relative difference is 4.51e-3. The one percent and fifty percent points, 2.407476 and 13.152901 psig against 2.4 and 13.1, stand as printed evidence and the overpressure probit is graded.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-probits', 15, 'Digest section 34 legislates the words for t I^(4/3) and for the sum of C^n dt. Which pair does this course use?', '["The heat exposure for t I^(4/3) and the toxic concentration for the sum of C^n dt, as the probit presets print them", "The thermal dose for t I^(4/3) and the toxic load for the sum of C^n dt", "The thermal load for t I^(4/3) and the toxic intake for the sum of C^n dt", "The heat product for t I^(4/3) and the concentration time for the sum of C^n dt"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 27 calls t I^(4/3) the thermal dose, and section 29 calls the sum of C^n dt the toxic load; section 34 binds every lesson and bank to those qualified words, because the bare word already means an occupational hygiene quantity elsewhere in the academy. The other pairs are not the course''s terms.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 1, '`TOXIC_PROBITS` computes Y = a + b ln(C^n t). In what unit does every toxic preset take the exposure time t?', '["Minutes", "Seconds, the same unit as the thermal probits use", "Hours", "Seconds for the Lees presets and minutes for the Purple Book ones"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 29 states t in MINUTES for every toxic preset, and the unit table in section 2 pairs exposureMinutes with a toxic exposure. Seconds belong to the thermal probits'' exposureTimeS. No preset takes hours, and the two sources differ in their concentration unit, never in their time unit.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 2, 'Chlorine carries both a Lees preset and a Purple Book preset. In which concentration unit does lees-chlorine expect C?', '["mg/m3, the unit of every preset in the table", "mg/m3 at 298.15 K, converted inside the preset from the Purple Book coefficients", "ppm, as OSD/30 prints the Lees presets", "g/m3, as the Purple Book''s worked plume prints its concentration"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 29: the Purple Book presets (pb-) are in mg/m3 and the Lees presets, as OSD/30 prints them, are in ppm. So lees-chlorine takes ppm with a = -8.29, b = 0.92 and n = 2. The g/m3 figure is the plume case of section 11, and no preset converts between the two sources.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 3, 'Chlorine at 400 ppm is held for 10 minutes. What probability does the lees-chlorine preset give?', '["0.309443, which the Purple Book preset gives for the same exposure", "0.441437", "0.077409", "0.073459"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two source table prints 0.441437 under Lees at 400 ppm for 10 minutes, with a toxic load of 1600000.000000 (ppm)^2 min. 0.309443 is the Purple Book at the same exposure. 0.077409 and 0.073459 are the Lees and Purple Book figures at 200 ppm.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 4, 'At 100 ppm of chlorine for 10 minutes, Lees gives a toxic load of 100000.000000 and the Purple Book 58987241.122310. In what units does the engine print those two toxic loads?', '["ppm min for both, since the toxic load is a concentration held for a time and carries that unit", "(mg/m3)^2 min for both, after the Lees concentration is converted with the molar mass of 70.9 g/mol", "(ppm)^2.75 min for Lees and (mg/m3)^2 min for the Purple Book", "(ppm)^2 min for Lees and (mg/m3)^2.75 min for the Purple Book"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The toxic load is C^n t in the preset''s own unit, and the engine prints the unit with every result: n is 2 for Lees in ppm and 2.75 for the Purple Book in mg/m3. The load is not linear in C, Lees stays in ppm, and swapping the exponents mismatches each preset''s n.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 5, 'For the Purple Book chlorine preset, 50 ppm is converted at 25 C with a stated molar mass of 70.9 g/mol. What concentration goes into the toxic load?', '["289.796976 mg/m3, the conversion of 100 ppm", "579.593951 mg/m3, the conversion of 200 ppm", "1159.187903 mg/m3, the conversion of 400 ppm", "144.898488 mg/m3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two source table converts 50 ppm to 144.898488 mg/m3 for the pb-chlorine preset. 289.796976, 579.593951 and 1159.187903 mg/m3 are the 100, 200 and 400 ppm conversions, each proportional to the ppm at the same temperature and molar mass.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 6, 'Hydrogen sulphide at 800 ppm for 15 minutes is run on the pb-hydrogen-sulfide preset with a molar mass of 34.08 g/mol and no temperature stated. What probability does the engine give?', '["0.333853, which is the figure the engine gives when 293.15 K is stated", "0.322245", "4.538569", "4.570702"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With no temperature stated the engine converts at its default of 298.15 K: 1114.389950 mg/m3, a probit of 4.538569 and a probability of 0.322245. 0.333853 belongs to 293.15 K. 4.538569 and 4.570702 are the probits at the two temperatures, read as if they were probabilities.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 7, 'In the same hydrogen sulphide case, why does stating 293.15 K give a higher probability than the default 298.15 K?', '["A colder gas is denser, so the same 800 ppm is more mg/m3, 1133.397113 against 1114.389950", "The preset''s coefficients are fitted at 293.15 K and corrected for a warmer one", "The exposure time is scaled by the ratio of the two temperatures before the load is formed", "The molar mass is taken at 293.15 K, where it is larger"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine converts ppm to mg/m3 through the ideal gas molar volume at the stated temperature (section 12), and a colder gas packs more mass into a cubic metre, so the mg/m3 figure and the toxic load rise. The coefficients, the time and the molar mass are not changed by the temperature.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 8, 'A ppm concentration is passed to a mg/m3 preset with no molar mass. What does the engine return?', '["A result converted with the carbon monoxide molar mass of 28.01 g/mol, the course''s example gas", "A result using the ppm directly as if it were mg/m3", "A refusal: \"molarMassGMol: must be a molar mass above 0 g/mol\"", "A refusal naming `molarMassKgMol`: \"molarMassKgMol: must be a molar mass above 0 kg/mol (hydrogen is 0.002016)\""]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without a molar mass the conversion cannot be made, and the engine refuses on `molarMassGMol` with the message quoted. It never assumes a gas or skips the conversion. `molarMassKgMol` is the source term field in kg/mol, and the concentration functions take g/mol.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 9, 'A history of 150 for 5 minutes, 90 for 10 minutes and 30 for 20 minutes is passed to `toxicDose` at n = 2. What toxic load does it return?', '["211500.000000", "144642.857143, which is the toxic load of the time weighted mean held for the whole time", "64.285714", "25000.000000"]'::jsonb, 0, '`toxicDose` sums C^n dt over the history, "D = sum(C^n dt)", and the digest prints 211500.000000. 144642.857143 is the load of the time weighted mean, 64.285714, held for the whole time; 64.285714 is the mean concentration itself, and 25000.000000 is the Lees chlorine load at 50 ppm for 10 minutes.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 10, 'Why is the toxic load of that history, 211500.000000, larger than the load of its time weighted mean held for the same time?', '["The engine adds a peak factor of its own to any history containing more than one concentration step", "The history is summed in seconds while the mean is held in minutes, so the history reads larger", "The mean concentration of 64.285714 is rounded down before its load is formed", "With n above one a peak counts for more than its share of the average"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The load goes as C^n, and at n = 2 the 150 step contributes far more than a flat 64.285714 would, so the history gives 211500.000000 against 144642.857143. The engine applies no peak factor, both sums are in minutes, and nothing is rounded.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 11, 'The Purple Book''s worked carbon monoxide case, 21,300 mg/m3 for 30 minutes, prints Pr 5.97 and P 0.835. The engine gives 5.967660 and 0.833393. Why does the golden allow 0.002 absolute on the probability?', '["The engine''s normal CDF is good only to 0.002, which is why every probability in the course prints to three decimals", "The carbon monoxide coefficients are single route, and every single route quantity gets a wider tolerance", "The Purple Book reads its probability off Table 5.1, so the printed 0.835 carries the table''s resolution", "The plume that produced the concentration is accurate to 0.002"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 29: the Purple Book reads its P from Table 5.1, which is why the golden allows 0.002 absolute. The CDF is good to about 1.5e-7, which is why probabilities print to six decimals. The Purple Book toxic coefficients are never graded, so no tolerance of theirs is widened, and the plume is not in this comparison.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 12, 'What do the Lees columns of OSD/30 Table 2 contain, and how do they stand against the engine?', '["The toxic loads giving one and fifty percent for every Purple Book preset; all reproduce to the printed two decimals", "The ppm giving one and fifty percent at 5 and 30 minutes for 13 substances; all 52 values reproduce within one percent or one ppm", "The coefficients a, b and n for 13 substances, of which two do not reproduce and were left out", "The mg/m3 at 25 C that gives fifty percent at 10 minutes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 29 describes the Lees columns as quoted, 13 substances at two probabilities and two durations, 52 values, all reproduced through the engine''s inverse within one percent or one ppm. That is the evidence that makes the Lees toxic coefficients gradable. The columns are in ppm, and all 52 values reproduce.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 13, 'At 298.15 K and 101325 Pa the engine''s molar volume is 24.465404 L/mol. What is the CCOHS and NIOSH figure of 24.45?', '["The molar volume at 293.15 K, 24.055117 L/mol", "A different gas constant used by occupational hygienists", "The molar volume at 273.15 K, 22.413970 L/mol", "Its rounding, which the golden gates at one part in a thousand"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 12 says 24.45 is the rounding of 24.465404 L/mol and the golden gates it at one part in a thousand. 24.055117 and 22.413970 L/mol are the engine''s molar volumes at 293.15 and 273.15 K, and the engine uses R_J_MOL_K, 8.314462618, throughout.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 14, '`toxicDose` is called with an empty history. What message does the engine return?', '["\"history: must be a non-empty list of { concentration, minutes }\"", "\"dose: must be above 0: ln(0) has no probit\", since an empty history sums to a toxic load of zero", "\"targetConcentrationMgM3: must be a concentration above 0 mg/m3\"", "\"probability: must lie strictly between 0 and 1\""]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 3 tables the empty history refusal on the field `history` with the message quoted. The zero load message belongs to `probit`, the target concentration message to `plumeDistanceToConcentration`, and the probability message to `probabilityToProbit`.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-toxic-probits', 15, 'Chlorine at 200 ppm for 10 minutes reads 0.077409 under Lees. What does the Purple Book preset read for the same exposure?', '["0.309443, the Purple Book at 400 ppm", "0.008118, the Purple Book at 100 ppm", "0.073459", "0.003487"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two source table prints 0.073459 for the Purple Book at 200 ppm, close to Lees there, while at 400 ppm the two part to 0.441437 and 0.309443. 0.008118 is the Purple Book at 100 ppm and 0.003487 is Lees at 100 ppm. The two sources disagree for the same exposure, and the note that uses one shows the other.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 1, 'How does the engine turn a probability back into a probit?', '["It evaluates an exact inverse of the normal distribution, the same exact form the oracle uses to write the golden records", "It looks the probit up in the Purple Book''s Table 5.1 and interpolates between the two printed cells either side", "It bisects on the same approximate normal CDF it uses forward, the Abramowitz and Stegun 7.1.26 form from lib/stats", "It uses Newton''s method on the probit equation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 30: the forward CDF is the repository''s `normalCDF`, Abramowitz and Stegun 7.1.26 with an error of at most 1.5e-7, and the inverse bisects on that same approximate CDF. The oracle''s exact normal distribution is what the golden compares against, Table 5.1 is evidence and no lookup, and there is no Newton step.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 2, 'At a probability of 0.01 the engine''s probit is 2.673653 and the exact probit in the golden is 2.673652. How large is the difference?', '["-1.78e-14, which is the difference the inverse table prints at a probability of 0.5", "8.59e-7, a departure in the seventh decimal", "3.91e-7, which is the difference printed at 0.9", "0.139897, the difference in the toxic load"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The inverse table prints 8.59e-7 at 0.01, so the engine departs from the exact probit in the seventh decimal. -1.78e-14 is the 0.5 row, 3.91e-7 the 0.9 row, and 0.139897 is what that departure becomes once it is carried through the lees-chlorine toxic load.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 3, 'For the lees-chlorine preset the engine''s one percent toxic load is 149793.673220. What does the exact probit give, derived in the digest?', '["149793.673220, identical, since the toxic load does not depend on the probit", "0.139897, the size of the difference itself", "100000.000000, the Lees chlorine load at 100 ppm for 10 minutes", "149793.533322"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 30 derives 149793.533322 from the exact probit, a difference of 0.139897: a seventh decimal departure in the probit is magnified through the toxic load. The engine''s own figure is the thing being checked, 0.139897 is the gap between the two, and 100000.000000 is a forward load at a stated exposure.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 4, 'Given that small magnification, what does the course do with a thermal dose or toxic load read back from a probability?', '["Quotes it to the figures the approximation supports, and never grades one", "Grades it at six decimals like every other figure the digest prints, since the forward CDF is good to about 1.5e-7", "Grades it to the tolerance the golden allows for the Table 5.1 cells on a rounding edge", "Rounds it to the printed digits of OSD/30"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 30 ends: a thermal dose or toxic load read back from a probability is quoted to the figures the approximation supports, and this course never grades one. The forward accuracy does not carry through the inverse, the rounding edge cells are kept apart rather than graded, and OSD/30''s printed figures are the evidence the engine is checked against; they set no rounding for a read-back value.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 5, 'Why does this course print its probabilities to six decimals?', '["Table 5.1 of the Purple Book prints its probabilities to six decimals, which the course copies directly", "The forward CDF is good to about 1.5e-7 in probability, so six decimals are figures it supports", "Six decimals is where the lees-chlorine toxic load and its exact value agree to the last digit", "The OSD/30 lethal doses print six decimals"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 30: the forward direction is good to about 1.5e-7 in probability, which is why the course prints probabilities to six decimals. Table 5.1 prints probits to two decimals, the lees-chlorine loads differ by 0.139897, and OSD/30 prints lethal doses such as 960 and 2380.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 6, 'Why is two phase discharge not in the engine, and what does the analyst do instead?', '["The Yellow Book forbids a closed form for flashing liquids, so the analyst runs the liquid outflow through the hole and then adds a flash factor to the result", "The engine treats a flashing liquid as an ideal gas, tests the critical pressure ratio at the hole and returns the choked mass rate with a warning", "No public closed form was read with a worked example, and the Yellow Book models are numerical; a two phase model outside this engine is used", "It was dropped as too rare offshore"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 31 gives the reason quoted and the remedy: a two phase model outside this engine for a flashing liquid. The engine has no flash factor, never relabels a liquid as a gas, and the digest names no offshore frequency argument. None of the section 31 items is taught as computed.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 7, 'An instantaneous release needs a puff. What does section 31 record about it?', '["The puff sigmas were not in a source read, so the analyst models a short release with a puff model elsewhere", "The engine models it as a Gaussian plume with a very short duration and warns the caller that the result is an extrapolation", "The puff is in the engine but ungraded, because it is a single route quantity", "It is modelled by the multi-energy method"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 31 lists the instantaneous puff as not in the engine because its sigmas were not in a source read. The plume is continuous and has no duration input, a single route quantity is one the engine does compute, and the multi-energy method is itself absent.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 8, 'A release sits in a built-up area. What does the engine offer for urban dispersion?', '["The Briggs urban coefficients, selected by passing an urban flag to `briggsRuralSigmas` with the class", "The rural Briggs set with sz2 changed to 0.00015, the value printed since Briggs 1973", "The class F coefficients, the engine''s stand in for a built-up area", "Nothing: a sourced urban sigma_y was not available, so the analyst uses the rural set with care, or another tool"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 31: only the rural Briggs set is here, because a sourced urban sigma_y was not available. There is no urban flag, 0.00015 is the misprint ALOHA corrects in class D, and class F is the most stable rural class, a weather state that has nothing to do with buildings.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 9, 'Why does the engine carry no jet fire model?', '["A jet fire is a pool fire with a tilt of zero, which `poolFireSolidFlame` already covers when it is given a zero wind at the hole", "The only worked example read carries internal inconsistencies, so a golden could not tell a right engine from a wrong one", "A jet fire burns too briefly to harm the people exposed, so the engine leaves it out as negligible", "Jet fires fall outside the Yellow Book"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 31 gives the reason quoted, and the analyst uses a jet fire model elsewhere. A pool fire is not a jet, nothing in the validation record calls a jet fire negligible, and the digest says nothing about the Yellow Book lacking the subject.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 10, 'Why are the Kingery-Bulmash fits absent, and what does the analyst use?', '["The fits were tested against the conference column and failed it by more than 1.70e-4, so Kinney and Graham replaced them", "They are present but hidden behind the ground burst option that the caller sets on the charge", "They cover only Z above 40", "The coefficients were not obtained, since the sources read print curves; Kinney and Graham within its range is used"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 31 records the reason quoted. The conference column is evidence for Kinney and Graham and was never run against Kingery-Bulmash, the engine has no ground burst option, and the only thing borrowed from the Kingery-Bulmash compilation is the judged span of Z from 0.05 to 40.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 11, 'Lung, eardrum and structural probits do not appear in `OVERPRESSURE_PROBITS`. What does section 31 say?', '["They are folded into the hsc preset, which returns a combined injury probability for every blast target", "They are single route quantities, taught in this course with no graded answer", "Their source was not read, so they are not included; the analyst uses the fatality probit here and states what it covers", "They need an impulse, which the engine computes but does not export"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 31 names lung, eardrum and structural probits as out of the engine because their source was not read. The hsc preset is a single fatality probit from OSD/30 Equation 4a, section 32 lists no such single route quantity, and the fit returns a peak side-on overpressure with no impulse.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 12, 'A reviewer asks this engine for a point source heat radiation level and a setback distance. Where do those belong?', '["In the facilities engines, taught and graded by the Facilities courses on relief and flare systems and on layout", "In `poolFireSolidFlame`, which returns the setback as a distance to a heat flux alongside every step of its solid flame result", "In the later quantitative risk course, which grades individual risk and the F-N curve along with the frequencies behind them", "In `solidFlameHeatFlux`"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 22 and section 31: the point source model and the setbacks it implies live in the facilities engines, this engine does not re-expose them, and its validation record carries a test that asserts it exports none. The solid flame functions are this course''s own model and return no setback. Individual risk and the F-N curve are a different seam.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 13, 'What does the engine say about frequencies and risk, and which course do individual risk, PLL and the F-N curve belong to?', '["It returns a frequency beside each probability so that the potential loss of life can be summed by the caller as part of the work of this course", "It computes individual risk from the probit result at each receptor, and the F-N curve is left to the Facilities courses on relief and on layout", "It leaves frequencies to the risk and change course, which in that course also grades the F-N curve and the risk matrices that its bands are drawn from", "It computes effects and no frequency; those measures belong to the later quantitative risk course, with frequencies taken from a risk study"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A consequence model says what happens if the release occurs. Section 22 places individual risk, the potential loss of life and the F-N curve in the later quantitative risk course, and the risk matrices in the risk and change course. The engine returns no frequency, computes no risk measure, and this course grades none of them.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 14, 'Section 32 asks what still catches a mistake copied into both the engine and its oracle. Which of these quantities IS graded in this course?', '["The Bagster transmissivity, backed by the Yellow Book pool fire''s printed transmissivity of 0.71474 from Hottel charts", "The Burgess burning rate, backed by the Babrauskas asymptote for hexane", "The Lees toxic coefficients, backed by the OSD/30 columns", "Thomas in still air, backed by the facilities engine it is imported from"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The section 32 row for the Lees toxic coefficients reads ''the OSD/30 columns (section 29)'' and ''yes''. Bagster, Burgess and Thomas in still air each read ''nothing: the transcription alone'' and ''no''. A single route quantity is taught and never carries a graded answer.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 15, 'Why is no Purple Book toxic preset graded, apart from its use as a teaching comparison?', '["Only carbon monoxide has a published worked case behind it; every other substance rests on the transcription, so graded toxic probits use Lees presets", "The Purple Book presets take ppm, which the capstones cannot supply without a molar mass for each substance", "Every Purple Book preset fails the OSD/30 columns by more than one percent", "The Purple Book presets have no source string"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 32 records one published worked case, carbon monoxide, and says every other substance rests on the transcription, so the row reads ''no''; every graded toxic probit uses a Lees preset. The Purple Book presets take mg/m3, the OSD/30 columns test the Lees presets, and each preset names its source.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 1, 'The chain from release to harm starts with the AMENAM methane line at an upstream pressure of 2000000 Pa. What release rate and regime does the engine report?', '["0.258180 kg/s, CHOKED, the rate at 500000 Pa", "1.032722 kg/s, CHOKED", "5.163609 kg/s, CHOKED", "0.129090 kg/s, CHOKED"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 33 opens the chain with 1.032722 kg/s, CHOKED, the 2000000 Pa row of the AMENAM gas table. 0.129090, 0.258180 and 5.163609 kg/s are the 250000, 500000 and 10000000 Pa rows: once choked the mass rate is linear in the upstream pressure, so each is right only at its own pressure.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 2, 'That rate is treated as a sustained release of carbon monoxide in the UBIT wind, class F. What concentration, in mg/m3, does the engine give at ground level 200 m downwind?', '["1272.709839, the UBIT 2 kg/s release in class D at 200 m", "1562.900663, the UBIT release in class F at 500 m", "4764.608684, the UBIT release in class D at 100 m", "4582.243793"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 33 prints 4582.243793 mg/m3 for the chained release, class F, at 200 m. The other three are UBIT''s own 2 kg/s release at other classes or distances: 1272.709839 in class D at 200 m, 1562.900663 in class F at 500 m and 4764.608684 in class D at 100 m. Each step of the chain is one engine call carrying its basis.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 3, 'Held for 30 minutes on the pb-carbon-monoxide preset, what lethality probability does the chain end at?', '["0.284726", "4.431141, the probit", "0.833393, the probability the engine gives for the Purple Book''s own carbon monoxide case at 21,300 mg/m3", "0.500000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 33 gives a probit of 4.431141 and a probability of 0.284726. Reporting 4.431141 gives the probit where a probability is asked for. 0.833393 is the Purple Book''s worked case at 21,300 mg/m3 for 30 minutes, a far higher concentration, and one half would need a probit of 5.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 4, 'The chain ends on a Purple Book toxic preset. What standing does that probability have in this course?', '["It is graded, because carbon monoxide is the one Purple Book substance with a published worked case behind it in the Purple Book", "It is graded once the concentration is converted to ppm for the lees-carbon-monoxide preset at 298.15 K", "It is a teaching chain: a Purple Book preset serves as a comparison, and every graded toxic probit uses a Lees preset", "It stands as a risk figure for the receptor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The brief lists any Purple Book toxic preset, other than as a teaching comparison, among what is never graded, and section 32 says every graded toxic probit uses a Lees preset. The carbon monoxide case is evidence for that one preset without making the chain gradable, a conversion changes nothing about the chain as written, and a probability of harm given the release is no risk figure.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 5, 'Which of these must a consequence note state, by section 33?', '["Every warning the engine returned, beside every model named by the method the engine reports", "The frequency of the release and the individual risk at the receptor, so that the reader can judge the consequence", "Only the final probability, since each intermediate step carries its own basis inside the engine", "The tolerance to which the capstone grades"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 33 lists what the note states: the release case, the regime, the weather and why, the receptor, every model by the method the engine reports, every stated input, every warning, the probit preset and its source, and what the engine did not model. Frequency and individual risk belong to the later quantitative risk course, the steps are the note''s content, and tolerances are no part of it.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 6, 'Section 33 asks the note to list every stated input that is not the engine''s own. Which list is it?', '["The gravitational constant, the gas constant and the ambient pressure, which the engine exports as named constants", "The Briggs coefficients, the Kinney and Graham constants and the Table 5.1 cells, which the engine exports", "The discharge coefficient of 0.62 and the Schmidt number of 0.8, which the engine fills in by default", "A pool thickness, a transmissivity, a TNT yield, a radiative fraction and a soot fraction"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The inputs section 33 names are the analyst''s judgements: a pool thickness (no spreading model), a transmissivity (stated for any graded heat flux), a TNT yield (the caller''s), a radiative fraction and a soot fraction. G_M_S2, R_J_MOL_K, ATM_PA and the Briggs and Kinney and Graham constants are the engine''s own, and neither the discharge coefficient of 0.62 nor the default Schmidt number of 0.8 is on the section 33 list.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 7, 'The surface emissive power, the thermal presets and the two toxic sources each give different answers for one case. What does section 33 ask the note to do?', '["Average the methods so that the figure reported does not favour any one source", "Say which method it used and why, and show the other beside it: the disagreement is information", "Report the most cautious method alone and leave the others out of the note so that no reader can pick a lower figure", "Report whichever method the engine returns first"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 33: WHEN TWO METHODS DISAGREE the note says which one it used and why and shows the other beside it. The engine does not average methods, hiding the others loses the information, and every result names its method in its basis, so there is no order to pick a ''first'' from.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 8, 'ERHA at a 4 m/s wind carries a surface emissive power of 30886.154395, 180128.456236 or 52025.691247 W/m2. Why must a note quote a heat flux with the method that made it?', '["Because the engine rejects a heat flux call that does not state the capstone''s own method", "Because only the mudan-diameter figure is valid at a 4 m/s wind", "Because the three methods give three different answers for one fire, so the heat flux depends on the method named", "Because the three figures are the same power in three different units, W/m2, kW/m2 and MW/m2"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 17 prints the three methods, mudan-diameter, radiative-fraction and radiative-fraction-soot, for one fire and says that is why every call names its method and a heat flux is quoted with it. All three are valid calls, no capstone method is involved, and all three are in W/m2.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 9, 'Which model does this course add that the facilities pool fire setback states it does not provide?', '["The point source model, which this engine re-exposes from the facilities engines for comparison", "A jet fire model", "The solid flame model, which is the only fire model this course grades", "The Bagster transmissivity, which the facilities engines leave out of their fire work"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 22: the facilities pool fire setback states it does not provide the solid flame model; this course adds it and grades only the solid flame. The engine re-exposes no point source model and carries a test asserting it exports none, no jet fire is in the engine, and Bagster is a single route quantity of this engine that the facilities setback has no bearing on.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 10, 'A manager asks for this consequence to be placed in a band of a risk matrix. Which course do the academy''s risk matrices belong to?', '["The risk and change course", "This course, since a lethality probability is already a band on the matrix''s harm axis", "The later quantitative risk course", "The Facilities courses"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 22 assigns the academy''s risk matrices to the risk and change course. This course computes effects in physical units and places nothing on a matrix. The later quantitative risk course owns individual risk, the potential loss of life and the F-N curve, and the Facilities courses own setbacks.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 11, 'Routine emissions, produced water treatment and tank vapour losses come up in a review of the same facility. Where does section 22 place them?', '["In this course''s Associate tier, since the Mackay and Matsugu pool evaporation already computes a tank vapour loss", "In the Gaussian plume, which the engine runs for a routine release when it is given a stack height", "In the later quantitative risk course, which carries every greenhouse and oil in water measure", "In other courses: this course models an accidental release and nothing routine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 22 says routine emissions and their accounting, produced water treatment and tank vapour losses are other courses'' subjects, and this course models an accidental release. Mackay and Matsugu is a pool evaporation rate for a spill, the plume models an accidental release even from a stack, and the quantitative risk course owns frequency and risk measures with no greenhouse or oil in water measure among them.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 12, 'A note states the weather and why. Why might it state class F for a ground level release of UBIT?', '["Because class F is neutral, the default the engine applies when no class is given, as its basis block records for the call", "Because at 500 m class F gives the highest ground level concentration of the six classes, 6.519887 times class D", "Because class F is the most unstable class, a sunny afternoon", "Because class F is the only class inside the 100 m to 10 km range where the Briggs sigmas carry no warning"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 11: the stable night-time class F gives 1562.900663 mg/m3 at 500 m, derived 6.519887 times class D, because the plume stays thin and near the ground, so a note says why it chose it. D is neutral and the engine applies no default class; A is the most unstable; and every class has the same warning range.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 13, 'UBIT at 500 m in class D gives 239.712839 mg/m3 on the centreline and 105.544135 mg/m3 at 50 m crosswind. Why must the note say whether its receptor is on the centreline?', '["Because the distance to a concentration is always a centreline distance, and at 50 m to the side the concentration is 0.440294 of it", "Because the engine refuses any receptor off the centreline, so an off axis receptor needs a different function", "Because the crosswind receptor sees more, 0.876998 of the centreline value per metre to the side", "Because the ground reflection doubles the off axis value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 13: the ratio to the centreline is exp(-y^2 / 2 sigma_y^2), 0.440294 at 50 m, and the distance to a concentration is ALWAYS a centreline distance, so a receptor to the side sees less. The plume accepts off axis receptors, 0.876998 is the ratio at 20 m and is less than one, and the reflection doubles the ground level value everywhere it applies.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 14, 'A note on BONGA''s blast lists what the engine did not model. Which entry belongs there?', '["The Kinney and Graham overpressure beyond 300 m, which the engine does not return for a 500 kg charge", "The TNT equivalent mass, which the engine leaves to the caller", "The burst is free air, so a ground reflection is not modelled, and the congestion the multi-energy method treats is not in the engine", "The distance for an overpressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 25: the fit is a FREE AIR burst and a burst at the ground reflects its shock and is not modelled; the multi-energy method, which treats congestion, is not in the engine. The fit is refused only outside Z = 0.05 to 40, so 300 m is simply the last printed row; `tntEquivalentMass` and `distanceForOverpressure` are both engine functions.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 15, 'Which statement of the chain''s harm result meets the consequence note''s standard for the probit preset?', '["A probability of 0.284726", "A probability of 0.284726 on a TNO carbon monoxide probit, rounded to two decimals so that no reader over-reads it", "A probit of 4.431141, from which a reader can work out the probability", "A probability of 0.284726 on the pb-carbon-monoxide preset, source TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999), for 30 minutes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 33 asks for the probit preset and its source; the preset names its origin so two sources cannot be confused, and the Purple Book string is TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999). A bare probability hides the preset, ''a TNO probit'' is ambiguous and rounding throws away figures the engine supports, and a probit alone leaves the reader to do the engine''s work.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Using the lowest yield in use that the Yellow Book reports, how many kilograms of TNT stand for the BONGA cloud?', '["5960.869565, the mass at the top of the reported band", "2980.434783, the mass at a yield of 0.1", "596.086957", "1192.173913"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 0.02 the equivalence returns 596.086957. The figures 1192.173913, 2980.434783 and 5960.869565 sit against 0.04, 0.1 and 0.2, because the mass grows in step with the yield the caller types.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'At 50 m from BONGA''s stated 500 kg of TNT, what is Z?', '["6.299605", "12.599210, the scaled distance at 100 m", "3.791181", "2.519842"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing 50 m by the cube root of 500 kg gives 6.299605. 12.599210 and 2.519842 belong to 100 m and 20 m, and 3.791181 comes out of the inverse search at 50000 Pa.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'Close to a charge, at Z = 0.5, the Kinney and Graham ratio over ambient is 39.512808. What overpressure in Pa does the engine report there?', '["39.512808, the ratio itself read as a pressure in Pa", "33739532.632458, the overpressure at Z = 0.1", "1008789.503793, the overpressure at Z = 1", "4003635.242148"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine multiplies the ratio by the ambient, 101325 Pa unless stated, and prints 4003635.242148 Pa at Z = 0.5. Reporting 39.512808 forgets the multiplication, and 33739532.632458 and 1008789.503793 Pa belong to Z = 0.1 and Z = 1.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'The Yellow Book cites a TNT blast energy of 4.19e6 to 4.65e6 J/kg, yet the engine accepts 4.0e6 to 5.0e6. What is the wider band for?', '["It lets the caller fold the yield factor into the blast energy, so that a cloud with a low yield can be typed as a low energy instead", "It catches a units slip, such as an energy typed in kJ/kg, while admitting every value the Yellow Book cites", "It covers the heats of combustion of the fuels in `POOL_FIRE_FUELS`, which the function also accepts as a blast energy", "It matches the conference paper''s printed constants"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 23: the TNT blast energy is refused outside 4.0e6 to 5.0e6 J/kg to catch a units slip, and the refusal message itself cites the Yellow Book''s 4.19e6 to 4.65e6. The yield is its own input, a heat of combustion goes in as Emf, and the conference paper''s constants belong to the overpressure fit.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Which constants appear in the engine''s Kinney and Graham model string?', '["800 in the numerator with a rise term at 4.5, and breaks at 0.049, 0.32 and 1.35, as the 2020 conference paper prints the formula", "808 in the numerator with breaks at 0.048, 0.32 and 1.3, the third break read to one decimal", "808 in the numerator with a rise term at 4.5, and breaks at 0.048, 0.32 and 1.35 under the square roots", "A single power law in Z, with no breaks and no rise term in its numerator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The model string reads ps/pa = 808[1+(Z/4.5)^2] / (sqrt(1+(Z/0.048)^2) sqrt(1+(Z/0.32)^2) sqrt(1+(Z/1.35)^2)). The conference paper prints 800 and 0.049 but its column follows 808 and 0.048; the third break is 1.35; and the fit is a ratio of polynomials with three square root factors.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Which law does the engine''s `scaledDistance` model string name?', '["Kinney and Graham free air scaling, Z = R / W^(1/2), as the overpressure fit states it", "Hopkinson-Cranz cube-root scaling, Z = R / W^(1/3)", "Kingery-Bulmash hemispherical scaling, which the engine borrows its judged range from", "The Yellow Book TNT equivalence, Q_TNT = alpha_e Qf Emf / Em_TNT"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 24 quotes the model string "Hopkinson-Cranz cube-root scaling, Z = R / W^(1/3)". Kinney and Graham is the overpressure fit that takes Z, a square root law would be the wrong scaling, Kingery-Bulmash lent only the range, and Q_TNT is the model string of `tntEquivalentMass`.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'A charge and a distance give Z below 0.05 m/kg^(1/3). What does `kinneyGrahamOverpressure` do?', '["Returns 33739532.632458 Pa, the value at Z = 0.1, as the nearest printed overpressure", "Returns a result with the warning the Briggs sigmas carry below 100 m", "Returns the ratio 332.983298 with a note that it is extrapolated", "Refuses on the field `scaledDistanceMKg13`, because Z lies below the lower edge of the judged range"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The range is Z from 0.05 to 40 and the engine refuses on either side of it, naming `scaledDistanceMKg13`. It never substitutes a neighbouring row, the warning and still return behaviour belongs to the Briggs sigmas, and 332.983298 is the ratio at Z = 0.1.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Searching for 5000 Pa on the 500 kg charge, how far out does the bisection land, in m?', '["141.169703", "79.281907, the distance for 10000 Pa", "200, the forward row where the overpressure is 3427.919093 Pa", "300"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The inverse table prints Z = 17.786268 and 141.169703 m for 5000 Pa. 79.281907 m answers 10000 Pa. At 200 m the forward table gives 3427.919093 Pa and at 300 m 2247.931733 Pa, both below 5000 Pa, so the target lies closer in than either.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'A 20000 Pa target on BONGA''s charge is solved on Z. Which Z comes out?', '["6.299605", "2.749433", "6.227368", "3.791181, the Z for 50000 Pa"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The search lands on 6.227368, which places 20000 Pa at 49.426650 m. At 6.299605, the 50 m forward row, the fit gives 19626.657230 Pa, slightly short, so the answer lies a touch inside. 2.749433 and 3.791181 answer 100000 and 50000 Pa.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'The conference case cbu-0.1kg-2m prints 38.71 kPa. What does the engine give for it, in kPa?', '["174.869459, the engine''s figure for cbu-0.1kg-1m", "38.707784", "9.065697", "29.238107, the engine''s figure for cbu-1kg-5m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The published column gives the engine 38.707784 kPa for cbu-0.1kg-2m, a relative difference of 5.73e-5. 174.869459 kPa is the 1 m case of the same charge, 9.065697 kPa the 5 m case and 29.238107 kPa the 1 kg charge at 5 m.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Nearest the charge, BONGA''s forward table has a 20 m row. Which Pa figure is printed there?', '["207926.999232, the ratio table at Z = 2", "100000, the target the inverse places at 21.822266 m", "19626.657230, the 50 m row", "121853.141070"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 20 m row prints Z = 2.519842 and 121853.141070 Pa. Z = 2 in the ratio table is a smaller Z and gives 207926.999232 Pa. 100000 Pa sits at 21.822266 m, a little farther out, and 19626.657230 Pa is the 50 m row.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Why can a distance the inverse returns, such as 79.281907 m for 10000 Pa, be trusted as far as the fit goes?', '["Feeding it back through Kinney and Graham returns the target, and that forward fit is the one the published conference column confirms", "The inverse is checked against the Kingery-Bulmash charts at every row of the BONGA table before it is returned to the caller", "The inverse is a closed form, so it carries no numerical error at all", "The Yellow Book prints the same distances"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 25 says every row round trips through the forward fit, and section 24 says the forward fit reproduces the published column, so Kinney and Graham forward and inverse is graded. Kingery-Bulmash is not in the engine, the inverse is a bisection on Z, and the Yellow Book prints no BONGA distances.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'Under P = Phi(Y - 5), what probability does a probit of 3 stand for?', '["0.001350, the probability of a probit of 2", "0.158655, the probability of a probit of 4", "0.022750", "0.977250"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ladder prints 0.022750 at Y = 3, two standard deviations below the mean. 0.001350 is Y = 2 and 0.158655 is Y = 4. 0.977250 is the mirror image at Y = 7.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Table 5.1 prints 7.33 for a probability of 0.99. What probit does the engine''s inverse give?', '["7.326348", "7.326347", "6.281552", "2.673653"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The printed table selection gives the engine 7.326347 at 0.99, which rounds to the printed 7.33. 7.326348 is the golden''s exact value, one digit away in the seventh decimal, 6.281552 is the 0.9 cell and 2.673653 is the mirror cell at 0.01.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Against a printed 2670, what fifty percent figure does the engine compute on the lees thermal preset?', '["2668.968204", "2376.626918, the Eisenberg lethal dose for fifty percent", "829.172848", "1046.414670"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lees at fifty percent comes out at 2668.968204, a whisker under the printed 2670. Among the wrong options sit Eisenberg''s fifty percent (2376.626918), the Lees one percent (829.172848) and Tsao and Perry''s fifty percent (1046.414670).'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'On the hsc overpressure probit, what probability does 2 psig give?', '["0.092574, the probability at 5 psig", "2.419612, the probit at 2 psig", "0.353660", "0.004934"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The overpressure probit table prints a probit of 2.419612 and a probability of 0.004934 at 2 psig, taken as 13789.514586 Pa. 0.092574 belongs to 5 psig and 0.353660 to 10 psig, and 2.419612 is the probit rather than the probability.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'Which psig does the hsc probit need for one percent harmed, where the source printed 2.4?', '["13.152901, the engine''s point for fifty percent", "2.419612, the probit the engine returns at 2 psig", "2.407476", "43.696383"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The printed points table gives 2.407476 psig at 0.01, a relative difference of 3.11e-3. 13.152901 and 43.696383 psig are the engine''s points at 0.5 and 0.95, and 2.419612 is a probit, a different quantity.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'At 20000 W/m2 for 20 s, what probit does the purple-book thermal preset give?', '["5.094507, the Tsao and Perry probit for the same exposure", "5.092979", "2.994507, the Eisenberg probit", "1.666556"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 27 gives 5.092979 for the Purple Book form, next to Tsao and Perry''s 5.094507, because the Purple Book''s -36.38 is Tsao and Perry''s -36.378 rounded. 2.994507 is Eisenberg, and 1.666556 is Eisenberg in W/m2 at 10000 W/m2 for 30 s.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, '`probabilityToProbit` is called with a probability of 1. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["\"probability: must lie strictly between 0 and 1\"", "\"dose: must be above 0: ln(0) has no probit\"", "A probit of 8, the top of the ladder, where the probability is 0.998650", "\"coefficients: unknown preset ''tno''; one of eisenberg, tsao-perry, lees, purple-book, or give { a, b } explicitly\""]'::jsonb, 0, 'A probability of one has no finite probit, and the engine refuses on the field `probability` with the message quoted. The ln(0) message belongs to `probit` with a zero thermal dose or toxic load, the ladder is a table of stated probits with no ceiling, and the preset message belongs to `thermalProbit`.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'The four thermal presets take their intensity in different units. In what unit does the caller pass the heat flux?', '["In kW/m2 for every preset, since three of the four were printed in kW/m2 by OSD/30 Table 17", "In the unit of the chosen preset, kW/m2 for eisenberg and W/m2 for purple-book, with no conversion", "In (kW/m2)^(4/3) s", "In W/m2 for every preset; each preset converts it to the unit its source prints"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 27: each preset is fed the heat flux in W/m2 and converts it to its own unit, which is how the kW/m2 presets and the W/m2 Purple Book form can be compared on one exposure. The caller never converts, and (kW/m2)^(4/3) s is the unit of the thermal dose.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Ten minutes in 100 ppm of chlorine: what fraction harmed does the Lees preset predict?', '["0.008118", "0.000035", "0.003487", "0.077409, the Lees probability at 200 ppm"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Squaring 100 ppm and multiplying by ten minutes gives 100000.000000, and lees-chlorine turns that into 0.003487. Of the others, 0.008118 is pb-chlorine at that concentration, 0.000035 is a quarter as much load on the Lees side, and 0.077409 needs twice the ppm.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'On pb-chlorine, the weakest row of the two source table, 50 ppm, yields which probability?', '["0.000035", "0.000395", "0.008118", "0.003487"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Purple Book side converts 50 ppm to 144.898488 mg/m3, forms 8768505.854629 (mg/m3)^2.75 min and prints 0.000395. 0.000035 is the Lees side of that row, and 0.008118 and 0.003487 sit one row down.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Carbon monoxide at 50 ppm is converted at 273.15 K, 101325 Pa and 28.01 g/mol. How many mg/m3 does the engine give?', '["57.244099, the conversion at 298.15 K", "58.220461, the conversion at 293.15 K", "76.024017", "62.483354"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conversion table prints 62.483354 mg/m3 at 273.15 K, where the molar volume is 22.413970 L/mol. 58.220461 and 57.244099 are the same 50 ppm at 293.15 and 298.15 K, and 76.024017 is hydrogen sulphide at 273.15 K.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Hydrogen sulphide (34.08 g/mol) at 400 ppm and 293.15 K: what does `ppmToMgM3` return?', '["566.698556", "557.194975, the same ppm at 298.15 K", "608.192135", "465.763690"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine divides ppm times the molar mass by the molar volume at the stated temperature, 24.055117 L/mol at 293.15 K, and prints 566.698556 mg/m3. 557.194975 and 608.192135 are the 298.15 and 273.15 K columns, and 465.763690 is carbon monoxide at 293.15 K.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'Stating 293.15 K for hydrogen sulphide at 800 ppm for 15 minutes on pb-hydrogen-sulfide, what probit does the engine give?', '["4.538569, the probit at the default 298.15 K", "5.967660, the Purple Book carbon monoxide probit", "4.570702", "4.431141"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 293.15 K the concentration is 1133.397113 mg/m3 and the probit 4.570702, for a probability of 0.333853. 4.538569 is the default 298.15 K result, 5.967660 the carbon monoxide worked case and 4.431141 the carbon monoxide chain of section 33.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'Appendix 6.B step 4.3 of the Purple Book prints Pr 5.97. What does the engine''s own probit come to?', '["5.97", "5.967660", "4.431141, the chain''s carbon monoxide probit at 30 minutes", "5.092979"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine computes 5.967660, which rounds to the printed figure. 5.97 is the book''s value itself, 4.431141 comes from a far weaker exposure in section 33, and 5.092979 belongs to the heat probits.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Which Lees preset shares b = 3.008 and n = 1.43 with lees-hydrogen-sulphide?', '["lees-hydrogen-cyanide, with a = -29.42 against -31.42", "lees-hydrogen-fluoride, whose b in the Lees table is 3.354", "pb-hydrogen-sulfide, the Purple Book preset for the same gas, with n = 1.9", "lees-phosgene"]'::jsonb, 0, '`TOXIC_PROBITS` lists lees-hydrogen-sulphide at -31.42, 3.008, 1.43 and lees-hydrogen-cyanide at -29.42, 3.008, 1.43. lees-hydrogen-fluoride has b = 3.354 and n = 1, pb-hydrogen-sulfide is in mg/m3 with b = 1 and n = 1.9, and lees-phosgene has b = 3.686 and n = 1.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'The plume returns a concentration in mg/m3. When does it also give ppm, and how do the two conversion functions relate?', '["Always, at 24.45 L/mol, the CCOHS figure the engine uses in place of its own molar volume for every conversion", "Only in class D, since the other classes print mg/m3 alone", "Never, since ppm is a probit unit", "Only when a molar mass is given; `ppmToMgM3` and `mgM3ToPpm` are exact inverses at the same temperature and pressure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 12: the plume returns mg/m3, and ppm only when a molar mass is given; the two functions are exact inverses at the same temperature and pressure. The engine computes R T / P and 24.45 is only the rounding of 24.465404 L/mol, every class prints both columns, and ppm is the unit of the Lees presets.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'In the table of engine against exact probits, what difference is printed at a probability of 0.5?', '["8.59e-7, the difference at 0.01", "-8.59e-7, the difference at 0.99", "-1.78e-14", "-3.91e-7"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At one half the engine''s probit is 5.000000 and the table prints a difference of -1.78e-14, effectively none, because the CDF is symmetric there. 8.59e-7 and -8.59e-7 are the 0.01 and 0.99 rows and -3.91e-7 the 0.1 row.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'A spill has no bund. What does section 31 record about unconfined pool spreading?', '["It is computed from the Mackay and Matsugu evaporation rate until the pool stops growing", "The Yellow Book spreading model is a differential equation for a fed pool, so only a stated thickness is implemented, and the analyst says where it came from", "The engine spreads the pool to 0.005 m, the thinnest stated thickness, and reports UNCONFINED_STATED_THICKNESS with the area and diameter that thickness implies", "The engine confines it on a default bund floor of 400 m2, the teaching stream''s bund"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 31: unconfined pool spreading is out of the engine because the Yellow Book model is a differential equation for a fed pool; `poolFromSpill` takes a stated thickness, and the note says where it came from. Evaporation does not size the pool, 0.005 m is only one row of a teaching sweep, and the engine carries no default bund, refusing only when neither a bund nor a thickness is given.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Why is the TNO multi-energy method not in the engine?', '["Its blast charts are graphical and no published curve fit was found, so congestion is treated with a method outside this engine", "It is a frequency method for vapour cloud explosions, and every frequency belongs to the later quantitative risk course and its own studies", "It gives the same answer as Kinney and Graham at a yield of 0.1", "It needs a Kingery-Bulmash fit to run"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 31 gives the reason quoted and the remedy. The multi-energy method is a blast method for congestion and carries no frequency; nothing in the digest equates it with Kinney and Graham at any yield; and its absence has nothing to do with Kingery-Bulmash.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Mackay and Matsugu evaporation is taught in this course. Why is it never a graded answer?', '["It refuses calm air, so a graded field could not be written for every weather the capstones use", "Its constant 0.004786 is a misprint in the Yellow Book that the golden records as an erratum", "It is not in the engine", "It is a single route quantity: nothing catches a mistake copied into engine and oracle except the transcription"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 32 lists Mackay and Matsugu with ''nothing: the transcription alone'' and ''no''. The calm air refusal is part of its form, 0.004786 is the constant as exported with no erratum recorded, and `poolEvaporationMackayMatsugu` is an engine function.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Thomas''s flame length is in the engine twice. Which of the two is graded, and why?', '["Thomas in still air, because it is imported from the facilities engine and so has been checked twice", "Both, because the two forms agree at no wind, 35.746382 m", "Thomas with wind, because the Yellow Book pool fire stands behind it; Thomas in still air has only its transcription", "Neither"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 32: Thomas with wind, tilt and the surface emissive power are backed by the Yellow Book pool fire and graded; Thomas in still air reads ''nothing: the transcription alone''. Being imported does not make a second route, and the two forms disagree at no wind, 35.746382 m against 37.101102 m for ERHA.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'How does the engine''s validation record decide which quantities can be graded?', '["It grades every quantity the golden carries, since the golden was written by an independent Python oracle", "It plants the same mistake in the engine and its oracle and asks whether anything independent, a second route or a published worked number, still catches it", "It grades a quantity when the engine and the oracle agree to twelve decimals", "It grades what the Yellow Book prints"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 32 describes the planted mistake test: a mistake copied into both engine and oracle is caught only by a second route or a published worked number. Agreement between engine and oracle cannot catch a shared mistake, which is the point, and the Yellow Book stands behind only some rows.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Which of these belongs in a consequence note for the chain''s first step, the AMENAM gas line?', '["The release case with its hole, pressure and temperature, and the regime the engine reported", "The release rate alone, 1.032722 kg/s, since the regime is an internal label with no effect on the rest of the chain", "The frequency of a leak of that size", "The Facilities courses'' setback for that line"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 33 opens the note''s list with the release case and its hole, pressure and temperature, then the regime. The regime is reported and stated. A frequency belongs to the later quantitative risk course and a setback to the Facilities courses, and this course computes neither.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'A client asks for the potential loss of life implied by the chain''s probability of 0.284726. What does this course do?', '["Multiplies the probability by the number of people at the receptor and reports the PLL", "Converts the probability to an F-N point using the Table 5.1 probit", "Reports the probit 4.431141 as the PLL", "Names PLL as belonging to the later quantitative risk course and computes none; the probability is an effect given the release"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 22 places individual risk, the potential loss of life and the F-N curve in the later quantitative risk course; this course never computes or grades one. A probit is not a PLL, and building an F-N point needs frequencies the engine never has.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'The Gaussian plume step of a chain carries its basis. At 500 m in class D, which citation does the plume report?', '["TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999) Appendix 6.B, the worked plume at 1 m", "TNO Yellow Book CPR 14E (2005), which the engine names as the source of the Briggs rural dispersion coefficients it uses", "NOAA TM NOS OR&R 43, ALOHA Technical Documentation (2013) section 4.3; sigmas Briggs rural (ALOHA Table 13)", "CCOHS, Converting occupational exposure limits from mg/m3 to ppm (24.45 L/mol at 25 C, 760 torr)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 4 quotes this basis for the plume at 500 m in class D, so the result carries its own citation. The Purple Book''s Appendix 6.B is the published check, the Yellow Book fixes the source terms and fires, and CCOHS fixes the ppm conversion.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Why does a consequence note state the TNT yield it used?', '["The engine picks a yield from the fuel name, and the note records the engine''s choice", "The yield is the analyst''s input, and the TNT equivalence behind it is a single route quantity with nothing but its transcription to catch a mistake", "The yield is fixed at 0.1 by the Yellow Book, and the note confirms that value was used", "The yield sets the ambient pressure that the overpressure fit multiplies its ratio by"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 33 lists a TNT yield among the stated inputs that are not the engine''s own, and section 32 lists the TNT equivalence as single route, which is why a graded blast states its TNT mass. The engine carries no yield default, the Yellow Book reports 0.02 to 0.2 in use, and the ambient is its own input, 101325 Pa unless stated.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'A note reports a thermal lethality of 0.953436 on Eisenberg for 35000 W/m2 for 40 s. What does section 33 ask it to show beside that figure?', '["The other presets for the same exposure, such as Lees at 0.858677, with the reason for the choice", "Nothing, since Eisenberg is the only thermal preset the capstones grade", "A societal risk figure for the facility, so that the reader has the full picture", "The thermal dose in W/m2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 33 asks a note to name the method it used and why, and to show the other beside it, since the disagreement is information; the preset table prints Lees at 0.858677 and Tsao and Perry at 0.999921 for this exposure. Societal risk belongs to the later quantitative risk course, and the thermal dose is in s (kW/m2)^(4/3) for Eisenberg.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'The chain treated a methane line''s release as carbon monoxide in the UBIT wind. What must its note state about the receptor?', '["Only the distance of 200 m, since the concentration is the same at every height", "The receptor''s individual risk", "Nothing about the receptor", "Its height and whether it is on the centreline, since the chain''s 4582.243793 mg/m3 is a ground level centreline value"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 33 lists the receptor height and whether it is on the centreline among what a note states, and the chain''s value is at ground level on the centreline 200 m downwind. The concentration changes with height and with crosswind distance, and individual risk belongs to the later quantitative risk course.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Which of these words may appear in this course only where a seam to another course is named?', '["Thermal dose, alongside toxic load and lethal dose", "Heat flux, alongside burning flux and evaporation flux", "ALARP, alongside individual risk, PLL and F-N", "k beta"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 34''s row on risk says individual risk, PLL, F-N and ALARP appear only where the seam is named. Thermal dose, toxic load and lethal dose are the qualified words the course requires, heat flux and burning flux are required forms too, and k beta is the Babrauskas product.'),
  ('consequence', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Why is the chain''s second step, 4582.243793 mg/m3 in class F, an effect this course may state while a frequency of that release is not?', '["Because class F occurs on only a few nights a year, which the engine records as a frequency beside the concentration", "A consequence model says what happens if the release occurs; how often it occurs belongs to a risk study", "Because the concentration is graded and a frequency is only taught", "Because a frequency needs the TNT equivalence"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 1 and section 22: the engine computes effects and never frequencies, and the analyst takes frequencies from a risk study, which the later quantitative risk course teaches. The engine records no weather frequency, a frequency is neither taught nor graded here, and the TNT equivalence has nothing to do with it.');
